this:\r\n```\r\nsession.query(User.name == 'filter').update({'name': 'update'})\r\n```\r\nas you can see, it's a wrong version of:\r\n```\r\nsession.query(User).filter(User.name == 'filter').update({'name': 'update'})\r\n```\r\nand the emited SQL was the following, which updated the whole table.\r\n```\r\nUPDATE users SET name='update'\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe would like to know if there's a reason to allow this behavior and if there's not, maybe it'd be a good idea to block this kind of statement. \r\n\r\nWorking example:\r\n```\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.declarative import declarative_base\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String, create_engine\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import sessionmaker\r\n\r\nengine = create_engine(\"postgresql://root:root@postgres/test\")\r\nSession = sessionmaker(bind=engine)\r\nsession = Session()\r\nBase = declarative_base()\r\n\r\n\r\nclass User(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = 'users'\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    name = Column(String(50))\r\n\r\nBase.metadata.create_all(engine)\r\n\r\nsession.query(User.name == 'filter').all()\r\n# SELECT users.name = 'filter' AS anon_1 FROM users\r\n\r\nsession.query(User.name == 'filter').update({'name': 'update'})\r\n# UPDATE users SET name='update'\r\n```","closed_by":{"login":"sqlalchemy-bot","id":36047385,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MDQ3Mzg1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/36047385?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot/orgs","repos_ur